  ],
  "text": "One vegetable from the Andes did more to Europe's population than any grain ever managed. Grain grows above ground, which means an army can burn it on its way past. A potato is underground. Burn the field and the crop is still fine. And it produces more calories per acre than wheat, on worse soil. Europe's population climbed steeply within a century of taking it up. Ireland took it further than anyone. Much of the country lived on it. Not just on potatoes. Largely on one variety of potato. So when a blight arrived that beat that variety, it beat the entire food supply. A million people died. A million more left and never came back. The same mistake as the banana, a century earlier and far more expensive. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
